The prompt’s key phrase is “valid subsequence”: the target property is existence of the ordered bracket subsequence `[[]]`, not merely reaching depth two in an unfinished suffix. That interpretation explains the otherwise tricky negative example ending in many opens plus only one close. I’m shaping the implementation as a four-state scan, which keeps both the Python and K proof small and faithful.
